.TH CCOPY 1 "2024-01-15" "ctools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccopy \- copy a conversation from one agent to a new session in another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccopy
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I source
.I destination
.br
.B ccopy
.I "FILE | /dev/fd/N | -"
.I destination
.br
.B ccopy
.B \-\-export-json
.I agent/session_id
.br
.B ccopy
.B \-\-import-json
.I agent
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccopy
(context copy) copies an entire conversation from a source session into a
brand-new session in a destination agent. The source session is never modified,
so it is a true copy, not a move.
.PP
The source is an
.I agent/session_id
(with no @ prefix), a conversation JSON
.IR FILE ,
an fd from process substitution
(\fB/dev/fd/N\fR), or
.B -
for stdin. The destination is a bare agent name; a fresh session is created
there holding the copied conversation, and ccopy prints the command to resume
it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Show what would happen without writing anything.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Structured logging to stderr.
.TP
.BI \-\-export-json " agent/session_id"
Print the session's conversation as a JSON list of
.BR {"role", "content"}
objects on stdout, and exit. A local-pipe convenience for scripting.
.TP
.BI \-\-import-json " agent"
Read a conversation JSON from stdin, create a new session in
.IR agent ,
and print the new session id on stdout. A local-pipe convenience for scripting.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I source
The source session, as
.I agent/session_id
(e.g.
.IR opencode/ses_abc123 ),
or
.I ssh://[user@]host[:port]/agent/session_id
for a session on a remote host. It may instead be a conversation JSON
.IR FILE ,
/dev/fd/N
(a process substitution), or
.B -
(stdin) — this is what makes
.B ccopy <(ssh remote ccat AGENT/SES) AGENT
work.
.TP
.I destination
A bare agent name (e.g.
.IR claude-code ),
or
.I ssh://[user@]host[:port]/agent
for a remote host. A new session is created in that agent.
.PP
Only agents that support session creation work as destinations
(claude-code, opencode, kilo, codex, pi). The others refuse with a clean error.
.SH "CROSS-HOST"
Either side may be remote, addressed as
.IR ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path .
ccopy runs over your normal ssh setup (keys,
.I ~/.ssh/config
, jump hosts).
.PP
The remote host does
.B not
need ctxttools installed. ccopy works in
two tiers: it first probes whether the remote runs ctools. If it does, it takes
the cheap path: the far side runs ctools itself and only the conversation JSON
crosses the wire (the source is read with
.B ccat
and
.BR ccopy ,
the destination seeded with
.BR ccopy ).
If it does not, it takes the expensive
path: it pulls the remote agent's storage (its sessions directory or its
database) over ssh with a plain
tar pipe, unpacks it into a local scratch directory, reads or writes the
conversation with the normal local agent code, and pushes any changed storage
back the same way. In that path nothing ctools-specific runs on the far side,
so a bare remote (just
.B sshd
and a POSIX
.BR tar )
still works. The storage location is anchored at the remote's $HOME
(the agent's default install root, e.g.
.IR ~/.local/share/opencode ,
.IR ~/.pi/agent ),
so the remote just needs whatever agent it already runs.
.PP
If the remote already runs ctools, the simpler Unix-y form is to pipe it: run
.B ccat
on the remote (it prints the conversation as JSON) and feed that into a local
.BR ccopy .
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccopy <(ssh chris@remote ccat opencode/ses_abc) codex
ssh chris@remote ccat opencode/ses_abc | ccopy - codex
.fi
.RE
.PP
Locally the wire form reduces to:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccopy --export-json opencode/ses_abc | ccopy --import-json codex
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy an opencode session to a new Claude Code session:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 claude-code
Copied 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new claude-code session: 1a2b3c
Resume it with:
  claude-code --resume 1a2b3c
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview without writing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 codex --dry-run
Would copy 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new codex session.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy a conversation to a remote host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 ssh://chris@remote/codex
Copied 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new codex session on chris@remote: 1a2b3c
Resume it on chris@remote with:
  codex resume 1a2b3c
.fi
.RE
.PP
Seed a new session from a conversation piped over ssh (the remote runs ccat):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy <(ssh chris@remote ccat opencode/ses_abc123) codex
Copied 42 message(s) from /dev/fd/63 to a new codex session: 1a2b3c
Resume it with:
  codex resume 1a2b3c
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Unknown agent, session not found, no conversation, or unsupported destination.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cdir (1),
.BR ccat (1),
.BR cextract (1),
.BR cgrep (1),
.BR cdu (1)
.SH AUTHORS
ctools project.
